  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA slow-moving front continues to slowly drift to the south\nacross the Ohio Valley, including across parts of northern and\nnortheast OH. Periodic showers and storms are expected through\nthe day today into tonight, with locally heavy rain and isolated\nflooding remaining the primary concern. Anomalous PWs on the\norder of 200+% of seasonal norms remain draped across the\nregion. This, combined with very deep warm cloud depths, provide\nan environment conducive for torrential downpours and localized\nflooding, especially in the more persistent or training\nactivity. This is most likely across parts of northwest/west-\ncentral Ohio through this evening, but the greatest coverage\nwill spread east into north-central Ohio late this evening into\ntonight.\n\nThere is also the potential for a few strong to severe storms\ntoday as well, primarily late afternoon through this evening\nnear/south of US Highway 224 into parts of north-central and\nnorthwest OH. Given the steep low level lapse rates but rather  \nmarginal midlevel lapse rates, the primary concern with this \nactivity will be strong to isolated damaging wind gusts, \nespecially from 21z to 03z. It is important to note that \nscattered to numerous storms are expected to persist past 03z, \nalthough the severe risk will be waning late into the evening. \nThe heavy rain and flash flood potential will probably increase \na bit after dark through the overnight with fairly widespread \ncoverage expected during this time frame, particularly for\nnorth-central OH.\n\nAlthough there may be subtle episodic wobbles in the front \nposition into the day Thursday, very little pronounced movement\nin the moist airmass is expected until Thursday night into \nearly Friday. The front will sag south Thursday night as \nCanadian high pressure builds overhead from the north. The \nforecast is trending drier Thursday night through Friday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nHigh pressure and tranquil weather will build into northern and\nnortheast OH Thursday night through the day Friday, with drier\nair and seasonable temperatures.\n\nThe front will surge back to the north into the daytime\nSaturday, bringing anomolously-high PWs back north with it. \nThis will allow for a reintroduction of shower and storm \nchances, particularly by late in the day into Saturday night as\nS/W energy works E through the southern Great Lakes. Widespread\nstorms are expected once again, particularly Saturday evening \nthrough the overnight, with some potential for activity to\nlinger into the daytime Sunday.\n\nThe min concern for this time frame will be the heavy rainfall \nthreat. Area PWs remain high with the potential for 1-3 inches \nof rainfall across Lower Michigan into Northern Ohio. WPC has \nmaintained a Slight Risk (Level 2 of 4) across northwestern and \nnorth-central OH for Saturday (ending 12z Sunday).",
